  <h1>Recognizing Elder Abuse and Nursing Home Neglect</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Reed Stafford (RS2), State APS &amp; Long-Term Care Reference. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>Recognizing elder abuse is harder than it sounds. Many of the warning signs &mdash; weight loss, falls, confusion, withdrawal &mdash; can also be consequences of underlying disease or normal aging. The pattern matters more than any single sign. So does context: a new bruise in a community-dwelling elder is usually nothing; a new bruise on an inner thigh of a wheelchair-bound nursing home resident is something. This guide walks through what to look for, how to interpret the CMS F-Tag system that governs nursing home regulation, and what an Adult Protective Services investigation actually involves.</p>

  <h2>Warning signs by abuse category</h2>

  <h3>Physical abuse</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Unexplained bruises, especially in patterns suggesting grip marks, slap marks, or restraint use.</li>
    <li>Bruising in unusual locations: inner thighs, inner upper arms, behind the ears.</li>
    <li>Fractures inconsistent with the reported mechanism.</li>
    <li>Burns in patterns suggesting cigarettes, immersion, or chemical exposure.</li>
    <li>The elder flinching when a particular caregiver approaches.</li>
    <li>Sudden, unexplained behavioral change &mdash; withdrawal, agitation, fear of specific staff.</li>
    <li>Restraint marks on wrists or ankles.</li>
    <li>Bedsores in someone who was previously mobile (suggesting inappropriate restraint or unjustified immobilization).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Sexual abuse</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Unexplained bruising in genital area, inner thighs, or breasts.</li>
    <li>Bloody, torn, or stained underclothing.</li>
    <li>Sexually transmitted infections.</li>
    <li>Sudden difficulty walking or sitting.</li>
    <li>Behavioral change &mdash; particularly fear of bathing, undressing, or being alone with specific staff.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Emotional and psychological abuse</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Withdrawal from previously-enjoyed activities or visits.</li>
    <li>Visible fear of a particular caregiver or family member.</li>
    <li>The caregiver speaking for the elder in a controlling way, or refusing to leave the elder alone with the family or APS investigator.</li>
    <li>Self-soothing behaviors that are new (rocking, mumbling).</li>
    <li>The elder describing being yelled at, humiliated, or isolated.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Neglect</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Unexplained weight loss (a clinically significant threshold is 5% in 30 days or 10% in 6 months).</li>
    <li>Dehydration signs: dry mouth, sunken eyes, decreased urine output.</li>
    <li>Untreated medical conditions or untreated pain.</li>
    <li>Poor hygiene: soiled clothes, unbathed, untrimmed nails, untreated nails fungal infections.</li>
    <li>Pressure ulcers (any new bedsore in a nursing home resident is a red flag, particularly if the facility&rsquo;s response is "they came in with it" without documentation).</li>
    <li>Soiled or wet bedding.</li>
    <li>Untreated wounds, infections, or dental problems.</li>
    <li>Medications not being administered as ordered (check the MAR &mdash; medication administration record).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Financial exploitation</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Unexplained withdrawals from accounts.</li>
    <li>Sudden changes to wills, trusts, beneficiary designations, or property deeds, particularly when the elder has cognitive impairment.</li>
    <li>Missing checks, credit cards, or valuables.</li>
    <li>Unpaid bills despite the elder having adequate resources.</li>
    <li>A new "best friend" or family member who has assumed control of finances.</li>
    <li>Forged signatures.</li>
    <li>Pressure on the elder to add a name to accounts or property.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Self-neglect</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>The elder living in unsafe or unsanitary conditions.</li>
    <li>Untreated medical conditions in someone living alone.</li>
    <li>Inability to manage activities of daily living (eating, bathing, dressing, toileting, transferring).</li>
    <li>Hoarding behavior creating safety hazards.</li>
    <li>Self-neglect is the largest category in APS caseloads. It is not typically civilly actionable against another party unless a caregiver or facility was on notice and failed to intervene.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The CMS F-Tag system</h2>

  <p>Every Medicare/Medicaid-certified nursing home is surveyed by state inspectors approximately annually. When the survey identifies a deficiency, it&rsquo;s coded by an F-Tag and assigned a "scope and severity" grade (A through L, with L being most serious). The deficiency report is publicly available through the CMS Care Compare website (medicare.gov/care-compare). For a family choosing a facility &mdash; or already trying to evaluate one &mdash; this is the single most useful public record.</p>

  <p>Key F-Tags to look for:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>F600&ndash;F610</strong> &mdash; Abuse, neglect, exploitation, and reporting failures. Any deficiency in this range is a serious warning.</li>
    <li><strong>F684</strong> &mdash; Quality of Care (omnibus).</li>
    <li><strong>F686</strong> &mdash; Pressure Ulcers.</li>
    <li><strong>F689</strong> &mdash; Free of Accident Hazards (falls, wandering, elopement).</li>
    <li><strong>F692</strong> &mdash; Nutrition and Hydration.</li>
    <li><strong>F725&ndash;F726</strong> &mdash; Staffing levels and competency.</li>
    <li><strong>F757&ndash;F761</strong> &mdash; Medication-related deficiencies (errors, unnecessary drugs, psychotropic restraints).</li>
    <li><strong>F812</strong> &mdash; Food Sanitation.</li>
    <li><strong>F880</strong> &mdash; Infection Control.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How to read the scope and severity grade</h3>

  <p>The grade combines two axes: how widespread the issue is (scope) and how serious the resulting harm was (severity). The grade letters are arranged in a four-by-three matrix:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>A&ndash;C</strong> &mdash; potential for minimal harm. Often minor, often quickly corrected.</li>
    <li><strong>D&ndash;F</strong> &mdash; no actual harm with potential for more than minimal harm. Common; should be tracked but not always alarming.</li>
    <li><strong>G&ndash;I</strong> &mdash; actual harm. Serious deficiencies; these matter for both regulatory and litigation purposes.</li>
    <li><strong>J&ndash;L</strong> &mdash; immediate jeopardy. The most serious classification; requires immediate facility action.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For a facility you&rsquo;re evaluating, any pattern of G-or-higher deficiencies is concerning. Any immediate jeopardy finding is a stop sign &mdash; even if "corrected." For a facility involved in your current claim, the survey history is among the first records an experienced elder abuse attorney pulls.</p>

  <h2>Adult Protective Services: what an investigation does</h2>

  <p>Every state has an Adult Protective Services agency (sometimes called Adult Services, Senior Services, or similar). When someone reports suspected abuse to APS, the agency conducts an investigation. The specific procedures vary, but the general arc is consistent:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Intake.</strong> The report is taken, often with the option to remain anonymous. Most states accept reports by phone (the Eldercare Locator at 1-800-677-1116 will route you to your state intake) and many accept online or written reports.</li>
    <li><strong>Triage.</strong> APS assesses urgency. Emergency situations may produce a same-day in-person response; non-emergency reports typically get an investigation within several days.</li>
    <li><strong>Investigation.</strong> An APS worker conducts a home or facility visit, interviews the elder (separately from any suspected abuser where possible), reviews records, and may consult with medical providers, law enforcement, and family.</li>
    <li><strong>Determination.</strong> APS classifies the report as "substantiated," "unsubstantiated," or sometimes "inconclusive." Substantiated findings are documented in the agency&rsquo;s records and may be reportable to professional licensing boards, criminal authorities, and (in some states) a registry.</li>
    <li><strong>Service plan.</strong> Where the elder consents (and has capacity), APS may help arrange protective services: alternate housing, in-home help, money management, legal services. Where the elder lacks capacity, APS may seek a court-appointed guardian or conservator.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>APS does not prosecute, sue, or litigate. Where criminal conduct is involved, APS refers to law enforcement. Where civil claims are warranted, APS findings become valuable evidence but the family or estate has to bring the civil claim through retained counsel. APS records are generally available to APS reporters and the elder; broader access varies by state.</p>

  <h2>Mandatory reporting</h2>

  <p>Every state has a mandatory-reporting law identifying categories of people required to report suspected elder abuse: physicians, nurses, social workers, paid caregivers, financial institution employees (for suspected financial exploitation), clergy in some states, and others. Most state laws grant immunity from civil and criminal liability to anyone making a good-faith report &mdash; even non-mandatory reporters. Failure to report by a mandatory reporter is itself often a misdemeanor and may support professional discipline.</p>

  <h2>If you&rsquo;re a family member trying to decide whether to report</h2>

  <p>The bar is low. APS exists specifically to investigate when family members aren&rsquo;t sure. Reports are typically confidential. Anonymous reports are usually accepted. Immunity is broad. The cost of an unfounded report is small; the cost of a founded situation that goes unreported can be devastating. Call the Eldercare Locator at 1-800-677-1116 if you&rsquo;re not sure what to do; they will help you think through whether to report and to whom.</p>

